  "text": "Clear the first SQL screen, and the follow-ups change. Rank these. Compare each day with the one before. Keep only the latest row for each customer. Nearly all of it comes down to one tool: the window function. There are twenty questions here. We'll work through twelve of them on real queries, then take the last eight at speed. Start with what a window actually is.\n\nQuestion one. What is a window function, and how does the over clause work? Group by collapses rows. Six sales by region become two totals: North four twenty-five, South six forty. A window function keeps every row. Sum over, partitioned by region, writes each region's total beside its sales: six rows in, six rows out. Inside over, you can say three things: which rows share a window, their order inside it, and the frame around the current row. Question two. What does partition by do, versus order by? Partition by splits the rows into groups, and the window starts over in each one. Order by sets the order inside a group, which is what a ranking needs. Rank, partitioned by region and ordered by amount, starts again at one for South. North's two fifty and South's three twenty both come first. Which brings us to ranking, and to ties.",
